WEBVTT
Kind: captions
Language: en

00:00:00.120 --> 00:00:02.880
Casting directors don't don't always get it right.&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:00:02.880 --> 00:00:06.660
From heroes of Middle-earth or&nbsp;
the most evil man in the galaxy,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:00:06.660 --> 00:00:12.240
these actors weren't the first choices for&nbsp;
their roles — but they were the best ones.

00:00:12.960 --> 00:00:18.540
Released in 2008, "Iron Man" was the film that&nbsp;
launched the Marvel Cinematic Universe. It&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:00:18.540 --> 00:00:23.520
introduced the iconic character of Tony Stark&nbsp;
to millions of people — a role that cemented&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:00:23.520 --> 00:00:28.920
Robert Downey Jr. as one of the most well-known&nbsp;
and highest paid actors in the MCU to date.

00:00:28.920 --> 00:00:33.900
In the first film, Tony's best friend James&nbsp;
"Rhodey" Rhodes is portrayed by Terrence Howard,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:00:33.900 --> 00:00:38.280
who was a real-life friend of Downey Jr. at&nbsp;
the time. Initially, Howard seemed like a&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:00:38.280 --> 00:00:44.160
perfect fit for Marvel, as he'd just come off his&nbsp;
Oscar-nominated role in "Hustle &amp; Flow" and his&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:00:44.160 --> 00:00:48.720
part in the Academy Award-winning film "Crash."&nbsp;
However, after the massive success of "Iron&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:00:48.720 --> 00:00:53.880
Man," Howard allegedly wanted a pay increase to&nbsp;
match that of lead Downey Jr. In an appearance on&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:00:53.880 --> 00:00:58.740
Bravo's "Watch What Happens Live," Howard revealed&nbsp;
that he had a three-film contract with Marvel,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:00:58.740 --> 00:01:04.260
and they were supposed to pay him $8 million for&nbsp;
the sequel. However, after all was said and done,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:01:04.260 --> 00:01:09.660
he was only offered $1 million, with the rest&nbsp;
of his salary going to Downey Jr. According&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:01:09.660 --> 00:01:13.620
to Howard, Downey Jr. wouldn't have landed&nbsp;
the role of Iron Man if it wasn't for him.

00:01:13.620 --> 00:01:19.140
Because of the drama, Howard was given the boot&nbsp;
and replaced by fellow "Crash" star Don Cheadle.&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:01:19.140 --> 00:01:23.700
Cheadle would go on to portray the character of&nbsp;
Rhodey-turned-War Machine in multiple other films,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:01:23.700 --> 00:01:29.460
including an upcoming lead role in Phase 5's&nbsp;
"Armor Wars." While Howard's performance was&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:01:29.460 --> 00:01:33.300
good in "Iron Man," Cheadle brings&nbsp;
a certain elegance to the role as he&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:01:33.300 --> 00:01:36.874
grows into more than just Iron Man's&nbsp;
sidekick throughout the franchise.

00:01:36.874 --> 00:01:39.780
"Hey, buddy! Didn't expect to see you here."

00:01:40.220 --> 00:01:43.080
"Look: it's me, I'm here,&nbsp;
deal with it. Let's move on."

00:01:43.080 --> 00:01:44.340
"I just—"

00:01:44.340 --> 00:01:44.760
"Drop it."

00:01:44.760 --> 00:01:45.720
"Alright."

00:01:45.720 --> 00:01:50.880
While "Iron Man" may have been the film to&nbsp;
launch the MCU, it was 2000's "X-Men" that&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:01:50.880 --> 00:01:55.620
helped to catapult the superhero genre back&nbsp;
into the pop culture stratosphere. The success&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:01:55.620 --> 00:02:01.800
of "X-Men" in 2000 can't be overstated: it&nbsp;
launched a franchise of ten X-Men films,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:02:01.800 --> 00:02:06.240
along with two Deadpool movies so far&nbsp;
and a spin-off in "The New Mutants."

00:02:06.240 --> 00:02:11.640
Anchoring the entire franchise is the&nbsp;
longtime presence of Hugh Jackman's Wolverine,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:02:11.640 --> 00:02:16.500
a character so popular that he essentially&nbsp;
became the main protagonist of most of the&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:02:16.500 --> 00:02:21.660
films. However, when "X-Men" was first being&nbsp;
developed, casting directors initially offered&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:02:21.660 --> 00:02:25.860
the part to Russell Crowe, who turned it&nbsp;
down. The role then went to Dougray Scott,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:02:25.860 --> 00:02:30.060
but due to scheduling conflicts with "Mission:&nbsp;
Impossible 2," he had to back out of the role.&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:02:30.060 --> 00:02:34.320
After that, the director turned to the&nbsp;
then-unknown Australian actor Hugh Jackman.

00:02:34.320 --> 00:02:40.020
"X-Men" is all the better for it. Jackman&nbsp;
has consistently put his all into the part,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:02:40.020 --> 00:02:46.800
culminating in a truly remarkable performance in&nbsp;
2017's "Logan." Jackman's portrayal is so loved&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:02:46.800 --> 00:02:52.260
that it's almost impossible to think of anyone&nbsp;
else playing Wolverine now that the X-Men are&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:02:52.260 --> 00:02:58.620
coming to the MCU — and it's only a matter of time&nbsp;
before Jackman reprises the role in "Deadpool 3."

00:02:58.818 --> 00:03:03.018
"Cause I'm the best there is at what I&nbsp;
do. And what I do best isn't very nice."

00:03:03.420 --> 00:03:08.700
Reshoots are very expensive by nature,&nbsp;
so the prospect of replacing an entire&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:03:08.700 --> 00:03:14.520
role — especially a central one — after a film is&nbsp;
nearing completion is almost unheard of. However,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:03:14.520 --> 00:03:17.760
following the shocking allegations of&nbsp;
sexual assault that were levied against&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:03:17.760 --> 00:03:21.900
Kevin Spacey in 2017, director&nbsp;
Ridley Scott made the decision to&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:03:21.900 --> 00:03:25.380
cut Spacey completely from his role in&nbsp;
the film "All the Money in the World."

00:03:25.380 --> 00:03:29.220
The actor had already filmed most of&nbsp;
his scenes for the film, but Scott&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:03:29.220 --> 00:03:34.140
hired celebrated star Christopher Plummer to&nbsp;
step in and portray the role of J. Paul Getty&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:03:34.140 --> 00:03:38.220
instead. Plummer began working on reshoots&nbsp;
of the Getty Oil Company founder right away,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:03:38.220 --> 00:03:43.320
as Ridley was determined to still meet the&nbsp;
movie's original release date. Not only was&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:03:43.320 --> 00:03:48.660
this a morally sound decision on Scott's part,&nbsp;
but it was also a boon for the film — Plummer&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:03:48.660 --> 00:03:53.506
was nominated for Best Supporting Actor at the&nbsp;
2018 Academy Awards for his work on the role.

00:03:54.001 --> 00:03:59.160
Michelle Pfeiffer's alluring performance as&nbsp;
Catwoman in Tim Burton's "Batman Returns"&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:03:59.160 --> 00:04:04.200
almost never happened. Initially, the role was&nbsp;
given to Oscar-nominated actress Annette Bening,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:04:04.200 --> 00:04:07.920
who had to suddenly drop out of the film after&nbsp;
discovering she was pregnant with her first&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:04:07.920 --> 00:04:11.820
child with her husband Warren Beatty.&nbsp;
According to Insider, the producers of&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:04:11.820 --> 00:04:16.140
the film were in a "panic," trying to find a&nbsp;
replacement for the role at the last minute.

00:04:16.140 --> 00:04:20.940
Bening had already been fitted for Catwoman's&nbsp;
skin-tight outfit — so the casting team was&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:04:20.940 --> 00:04:25.140
looking for people who could both act&nbsp;
the part and fit in the costume. Luckily,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:04:25.140 --> 00:04:30.060
Michelle Pfeiffer was not only the perfect size&nbsp;
for the costuming, but her performance in the&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:04:30.060 --> 00:04:35.400
role was a truly defining one for the character.&nbsp;
It's no wonder that she is consistently ranked&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:04:35.400 --> 00:04:40.500
as one of the best actresses to have ever&nbsp;
played the role of the whip-slinging antihero.

00:04:40.610 --> 00:04:45.740
"I am Catwoman. Hear me roar."

00:04:45.740 --> 00:04:51.180
Believe it or not, 1984's "Beverly Hills&nbsp;
Cop" was supposed to star not Eddie Murphy,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:04:51.180 --> 00:04:56.880
but Sylvester Stallone. The film was originally&nbsp;
meant to be an action-packed thriller.

00:04:56.880 --> 00:05:01.800
Stallone had a more brutal story planned for the&nbsp;
film, even suggesting that the character name be&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:05:01.800 --> 00:05:06.540
changed from Axel Foley to Axel Cobretti.&nbsp;
While this obviously never came to be,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:05:06.540 --> 00:05:11.880
the name Cobretti would later go to Stallone's&nbsp;
character in 1986's "Cobra." Meanwhile, the studio&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:05:11.880 --> 00:05:17.100
decided to go in an entirely different direction,&nbsp;
as Stallone's new suggestions would have bumped&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:05:17.100 --> 00:05:22.320
the cost of production way up. Just like that,&nbsp;
Stallone was out, and Eddie Murphy was in.

00:05:22.320 --> 00:05:26.460
Murphy's "Beverly Hills Cop" struck a&nbsp;
lighter tone, resulting in one of the&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:05:26.460 --> 00:05:32.340
most successful comedy franchises ever.&nbsp;
Two sequels released in 1987 and 1994,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:05:32.340 --> 00:05:35.280
and a much-hyped fourth installment is on the way.

00:05:35.940 --> 00:05:40.680
Nowadays, it's pretty much impossible&nbsp;
to think of Isildur's heir and the&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:05:40.680 --> 00:05:44.700
true king of Gondor as being anyone&nbsp;
other than Viggo Mortensen. However,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:05:44.700 --> 00:05:48.960
many people have been surprised to learn that he&nbsp;
only took on the role of Aragorn once shooting&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:05:48.960 --> 00:05:52.740
was already underway for "The Lord of the&nbsp;
Rings: The Fellowship of the Ring." Initially,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:05:52.740 --> 00:05:57.840
Peter Jackson's epic trilogy had Stuart Townsend&nbsp;
cast in the role — but after filming just four&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:05:57.840 --> 00:06:01.980
short days, Jackson reportedly decided&nbsp;
that the actor was simply too young.

00:06:01.980 --> 00:06:05.880
Townsend later revealed that he was&nbsp;
almost relieved when he was fired,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:06:05.880 --> 00:06:09.960
as his experience with the studio and&nbsp;
director was not a positive one. However,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:06:09.960 --> 00:06:14.220
he was still quite miffed that after months&nbsp;
of training and rehearsing for the role,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:06:14.220 --> 00:06:18.060
he never got paid for his time due to&nbsp;
the terms of his contract. Nevertheless,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:06:18.060 --> 00:06:22.800
once Viggo Mortensen was brought on to replace&nbsp;
him, the cast was able to work together to create&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:06:22.800 --> 00:06:27.360
the cultural phenomenon that was "The Lord of&nbsp;
the Rings." Thanks to Mortensen's portrayal,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:06:27.360 --> 00:06:32.130
Aragorn has become one of the most popular&nbsp;
characters from the blockbuster franchise.

00:06:32.130 --> 00:06:38.359
"I would have gone with you to the&nbsp;
end… into the very fires of Mordor."

00:06:38.359 --> 00:06:42.900
Before his appearance in "The Avengers"&nbsp;
in 2012, the character of Bruce Banner&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:06:42.900 --> 00:06:46.800
—more commonly known as The Hulk — was&nbsp;
initially portrayed by two other actors&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:06:46.800 --> 00:06:52.080
in major silver screen productions. First,&nbsp;
Eric Bana took on the role in Ang Lee's 2003&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:06:52.080 --> 00:06:52.837
film "Hulk." 

00:06:52.837 --> 00:06:53.520
— and this time,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:06:53.520 --> 00:06:55.800
Edward Norton portrayed the titular character.

00:06:55.800 --> 00:07:00.540
Norton was originally meant to keep the role when&nbsp;
the Hulk was brought into the larger story in&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:07:00.540 --> 00:07:05.580
"The Avengers," but there were some severe and&nbsp;
dramatic behind-the-scenes complications that&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:07:05.580 --> 00:07:09.600
lead to his recasting. The studio ended&nbsp;
up hiring Mark Ruffalo, who would go on&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:07:09.600 --> 00:07:13.920
to play the character in every subsequent&nbsp;
MCU film featuring the character thus far.

00:07:13.920 --> 00:07:18.600
Compared to the other iterations, Ruffalo's&nbsp;
Hulk is quieter in his demeanor. The actor&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:07:18.600 --> 00:07:22.200
brings out more of his loneliness,&nbsp;
intellect, collaborative spirit,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:07:22.200 --> 00:07:27.060
and kindness — all traits that were missing&nbsp;
in the other portrayals of the character. It's&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:07:27.060 --> 00:07:31.680
because of this that Ruffalo's Bruce&nbsp;
Banner has truly grown into his own.

00:07:31.680 --> 00:07:38.400
"I put the brains and the brawn together,&nbsp;
and now look at me. Best of both worlds."

00:07:38.940 --> 00:07:43.860
"Back to The Future" is another franchise&nbsp;
that has been famously plagued with various&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:07:43.860 --> 00:07:48.000
casting replacements. Fans of the franchise&nbsp;
know that Marty's girlfriend Jennifer Parker&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:07:48.000 --> 00:07:51.600
was portrayed by Claudia Wells in the&nbsp;
first film — but when it came time to&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:07:51.600 --> 00:07:55.500
shoot "Back to The Future Part II,"&nbsp;
she was replaced by Elisabeth Shue.

00:07:55.500 --> 00:07:59.280
There's also the very messy matter of&nbsp;
Jeffrey Weissman replacing Crispin Glover&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:07:59.280 --> 00:08:04.260
as George McFly. After a pay dispute with&nbsp;
the studio, Glover was replaced by Weissman,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:08:04.260 --> 00:08:07.500
who wore prosthetics to look like&nbsp;
Glover in the sequel film. Glover&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:08:07.500 --> 00:08:12.480
then sued over the use of his image without&nbsp;
his consent — and thanks in part to his suit,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:08:12.480 --> 00:08:17.280
studios have largely avoided using actors'&nbsp;
likenesses without permission ever since.

00:08:17.280 --> 00:08:23.280
However, the most notable replacement — and the&nbsp;
only one that we'd consider a great upgrade for&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:08:23.280 --> 00:08:28.020
the film — was when production decided to have&nbsp;
Michael J. Fox replace Eric Stoltz as Marty.&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:08:28.020 --> 00:08:32.100
"Back To the Future" was already four weeks into&nbsp;
production with Stoltz playing the film's lead&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:08:32.100 --> 00:08:37.140
character when director Robert Zemeckis decided&nbsp;
he just wasn't right for the role. Zemeckis felt&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:08:37.140 --> 00:08:42.060
that Stoltz was too serious, instead wanting&nbsp;
the project to be more light and carefree. Fox,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:08:42.060 --> 00:08:47.040
whose schedule was complicated by his commitment&nbsp;
to the hit series "Family Ties," eventually came&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:08:47.040 --> 00:08:51.240
in to reshoot Marty's already-completed&nbsp;
scenes and finish the film. The actor&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:08:51.240 --> 00:08:55.380
brought all of the desired energy to the&nbsp;
character, transforming the film into a&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:08:55.380 --> 00:09:01.296
full-on franchise and one of the most iconic and&nbsp;
influential sci-fi movies in cinematic history.

00:09:01.680 --> 00:09:06.540
The 2005 dystopian political drama&nbsp;
"V For Vendetta" initially starred&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:09:06.540 --> 00:09:11.340
actor James Purefoy in the role of V, an&nbsp;
eccentric masked vigilante who attempts to&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:09:11.340 --> 00:09:16.200
spark a revolution through acts of terrorism.&nbsp;
Purefoy spent six weeks filming the role of&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:09:16.200 --> 00:09:20.400
V before ultimately leaving the movie.&nbsp;
There were rumors flying around that he&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:09:20.400 --> 00:09:24.660
couldn't stand wearing the mask, but that&nbsp;
wasn't actually the case. Purefoy stated&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:09:24.660 --> 00:09:29.580
that both he and production agreed not to go&nbsp;
into details over why he left the project,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:09:29.580 --> 00:09:33.780
but he assured fans that it wasn't over&nbsp;
him having to wear a mask for the role.

00:09:33.780 --> 00:09:39.060
Once Purefoy left the film, Hugo Weaving stepped&nbsp;
in to replace him. While Purefoy may very well&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:09:39.060 --> 00:09:44.640
have done an excellent job if he'd stuck with the&nbsp;
role, Weaving's performance is downright perfect.&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:09:44.640 --> 00:09:49.800
The actor is able to embody the strength and&nbsp;
emotion of the character without using any&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:09:49.800 --> 00:09:55.440
facial expressions. There's a reason V's mask&nbsp;
has become such an immortal image, after all.

00:09:55.440 --> 00:09:57.660
"I'm not questioning your powers of observation,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:09:57.660 --> 00:10:01.868
I'm merely remarking upon the paradox&nbsp;
of asking a masked man who he is."

00:10:02.340 --> 00:10:06.900
While most actor replacements made way&nbsp;
into production require massive reshoots,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:10:06.900 --> 00:10:11.220
the one in Spike Jonze's sci-fi romance&nbsp;
"Her" required only minimal revision.

00:10:11.220 --> 00:10:15.360
Initially, it was Samantha Morton who&nbsp;
was cast to play Samantha, the faceless&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:10:15.360 --> 00:10:19.920
artificial intelligence that Joaquin Phoenix's&nbsp;
character finds himself falling in love with.&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:10:19.920 --> 00:10:25.260
Morton recorded all of her lines, and Phoenix's&nbsp;
acting was timed with her vocal rhythms, but it&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:10:25.260 --> 00:10:29.940
wasn't until after the movie wrapped that director&nbsp;
Jonze decided Morton wasn't right for the role.

00:10:29.940 --> 00:10:34.140
Jonze told Vulture that it was during the&nbsp;
editing stage in post-production that he&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:10:34.140 --> 00:10:38.700
realized Morton's delivery didn't create the&nbsp;
effect that he wanted. That was when he chose&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:10:38.700 --> 00:10:44.400
to recast the role with Scarlett Johansson, revise&nbsp;
the character, and bring back Phoenix to redo some&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:10:44.400 --> 00:10:49.560
of his own lines as well. Ultimately, this was&nbsp;
the right call, as Johansson's performance was&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:10:49.560 --> 00:10:54.660
highly celebrated — including a win for Best&nbsp;
Actress at the Rome Film Festival in 2013.

00:10:54.660 --> 00:10:59.340
Johansson would later use her voice acting&nbsp;
talents again in films like "The Jungle Book,"&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:10:59.340 --> 00:11:04.500
"Isle of Dogs," and "Sing 2." Thanks in part&nbsp;
to Johansson's involvement, "Her" is able to&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:11:04.500 --> 00:11:10.140
present a complex and compelling relationship&nbsp;
between Phoenix's character and his beloved AI.

00:11:10.740 --> 00:11:17.280
When "Star Wars: A New Hope" was released in&nbsp;
1977, Darth Vader was the villain of everyone's&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:11:17.280 --> 00:11:21.720
worst nightmares. So when it came time to&nbsp;
reveal that there was an even bigger baddie&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:11:21.720 --> 00:11:27.660
pulling Vader's strings in the follow-up films,&nbsp;
that casting had to be on point. Whoever played&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:11:27.660 --> 00:11:32.820
the evil emperor had to be more menacing&nbsp;
than Vader — a seemingly impossible task.

00:11:32.820 --> 00:11:37.920
In "Star Wars: The Empire Strikes Back,"&nbsp;
the emperor is voiced by Clive Revill and&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:11:37.920 --> 00:11:41.400
portrayed by actress Marjorie Eaton.&nbsp;
However, when it came time to really&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:11:41.400 --> 00:11:46.380
develop the character and have Luke Skywalker&nbsp;
face off with him in "Return of the Jedi," Ian&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:11:46.380 --> 00:11:51.720
McDiarmid was cast to play both his voice and&nbsp;
body. McDiarmid would continue to reprise his&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:11:51.720 --> 00:11:56.040
character in the prequel and sequel films.&nbsp;
Although it ended up being a subtle change,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:11:56.040 --> 00:12:00.480
it made all the difference — the intensity&nbsp;
and power that McDiarmid brings to the role&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:12:00.480 --> 00:12:05.940
is unmatched. Audiences can instantly detect&nbsp;
that there's something sinister about Palpatine,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:12:05.940 --> 00:12:10.140
but we can also see how he's so easily&nbsp;
deceived everyone around him. Without&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:12:10.140 --> 00:12:14.460
McDiarmid's portrayal of the emperor,&nbsp;
"Star Wars" would not be what it is today.

00:12:14.724 --> 00:12:19.104
"In time, you will call me Master."

00:12:20.160 --> 00:12:25.320
Director James Cameron's Alien sequel introduced&nbsp;
audiences to the likable Corporal Hicks,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:12:25.320 --> 00:12:30.480
a space marine and potential love interest for&nbsp;
Sigourney Weaver's leading hero Ripley. Initially,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:12:30.480 --> 00:12:35.280
Cameron cast James Remar in the role of Hicks,&nbsp;
but he was later fired and replaced by Michael&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:12:35.280 --> 00:12:39.900
Biehn. According to ScreenRant, Remar was&nbsp;
reportedly incredibly difficult to work with&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:12:39.900 --> 00:12:45.060
on set — and as such, his portrayal of Hicks&nbsp;
was a lot darker than was originally intended.&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:12:45.060 --> 00:12:49.680
Remar later admitted that he was suffering from a&nbsp;
serious drug addiction during the time of filming,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:12:49.680 --> 00:12:53.280
and that likely resulted in his being&nbsp;
let go and replaced in the project.

00:12:53.280 --> 00:12:56.220
Biehn was brought in as the&nbsp;
replacement fresh off of his&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:12:56.220 --> 00:12:59.520
success from "Terminator." He brought a&nbsp;
different perspective to the character&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:12:59.520 --> 00:13:03.420
of Corporal Hicks — one that aligned more&nbsp;
with Cameron's original vision. However,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:13:03.420 --> 00:13:08.700
if you look closely at "Aliens," there are still&nbsp;
a few moments where you can see Remar's Hicks,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:13:08.700 --> 00:13:13.500
as it was too expensive to reshoot and replace&nbsp;
every single one of his scenes with Biehn.

